const edges = `
<dialog class="sk-vaul" data-edge="inline-start">…</dialog>
<dialog class="sk-vaul" data-edge="inline-end">…</dialog>
<dialog class="sk-vaul" data-edge="block-end">…</dialog>`;

const vanilla = `import { connectVaul } from "@skryensya/vanilla/vaul";

// Just the drag. Open is showModal() and close is close():
// The modality is from the platform.
const cleanup = connectVaul(document.querySelector("#demo-vaul"), {
  // fraction of the panel to drag
  dismissThreshold: 0.4,
  // px/ms: a flick closes without crossing the distance
  dismissVelocity: 0.5,
  onOpenChange(details) {
    console.log(details.open);
  },
});`;

const noDrag = `<dialog
  class="sk-vaul"
  data-sk-vaul
  data-draggable="false"
  data-edge="block-end"
>
  <!-- without handle and no drag: Vaul is still complete -->
</dialog>`;
---

<Base title="Vaul" description="A pattern: modal panel anchored to a viewport edge, with optional drag-to-dismiss.">
  <h1>Vaul</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A modal panel anchored to a <strong>edge</strong> of the viewport: it arrives from that edge, leaves the page inert behind a backdrop, and goes back the way it came. The edge is the entire idea, a Vaul is named by where it comes from, never by the shape it makes when it arrives.
  </p>

  <ComponentPreview html={html} label="HTML" viewport="overlay" />

  <h2>Why is it a pattern and not a component</h2>
  <p>
    The rule is a single question: <em>could a second component need this exact structure? </em> Here the answer is not a prediction, it already happens, today, in this repo: <a href="/en/components/drawer">Drawer</a> <strong>is</strong> a Vaul on the inline edge. The <a href="/en/components/dialog">Dialog Vaul</a> option reuses the same block-end interaction on mobile without converting the Dialog into a Vaul component.
  </p>
  <p>
    So Vaul sends <strong>hooks and</strong> structure. A drawer that reimplemented the panel would be a second Vaul with a different name.
  </p>

  <h2>The edge is a data, not a component</h2>
  <p>
    Three edges, one pattern. <code>data-edge</code> choose; nothing else changes. The inline edges are logical, so they turn around on their own in RTL instead of sticking to the wrong side of the screen.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={edges} label="HTML" />

  <h2>Requires the native <code>&lt; dialog&gt; </code></h2>
  <p>
    The focus trap, ESC, inert background, focus restoration, top layer and a real <code>:: backdrop</code> are behaviors that the platform already has. A Vaul on a div reimplements them in JavaScript that would have to be sent, and the top layer and <code>: modal</code> are not available for a div at any price.
  </p>

  <h2>Zag is not used here, and that is the finding</h2>
  <p>
    The rule is that Zag comes in only when state coordination is worth its cost. The log was checked before writing a line: <strong>Vaul machine does not exist</strong>. And what Zag could cover is exactly what the platform already does best, <code>@zag-js/dialog</code> would reimplement the modality that <code>&lt; dialog&gt; </code> gives away, and <code>@zag-js/presence</code> is redundant against <code>@starting-style</code>. That the answer is "none" is the working rule, not an exception.
  </p>

  <h2>Drag is the only thing that costs JS</h2>
  <p>
    What remains <strong>is a</strong> behavior without an equivalent on the platform and without a machine in Zag: drag the panel towards its edge to close it. That, and only that, is the enhancer. <strong>Vaul is complete without it</strong>: without JS there is panel, slide, backdrop, ESC and click outside.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={vanilla} label="JavaScript" />
  <p>
    Closes by distance <strong>or</strong> by speed: a flick is an intention, and waiting for it to cross a distance threshold is exactly what makes a blade feel stuck. The speed is measured on the last <strong>millimeters</strong> of the gesture, not on the average: if you drag slowly, hesitate, and only then pull, the pull is real, averaged from when you put your finger down it dilutes until it seems like you did not move. And a <em>flick back to</em> beats him by distance: the last word of the hand is his word.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={noDrag} label="no drag" />

  <h2>For the other side, it resists</h2>
  <p>
    Pulling the panel inwards does not detach it from its edge, but it does not leave it stuck either: it gives way, tearing off 1∶1 under the bud and it curves to a stop of about 12px. That the finger moves and the thing below is not the only moment in which a direct manipulation surface gives itself away as a drawing of a surface. The <em>asymmetry is</em> the message: there is no path to that side.
  </p>
  <p>
    And what appears in that gap is <strong>plus panel</strong>: the material continues long past the edge, so lifting a sheet reveals a sheet and not a strip of backdrop. They are two different numbers on purpose, how much he gives (<code>--sk-vaul-overpull</code>) and how far the material goes (<code>--sk-vaul-material</code>), and the second is declared as a floor above the first so that giving can never beat him. The material is painted <em>over</em> of the elevation: in reverse, the shadow of the panel dyed just the stripe that was just revealed, and a raised sheet showed a dark band where its own surface should be, which is exactly the hole this exists not to have.
  </p>
  <p>
    The backdrop goes with that: its opacity follows the drag. A full backdrop with the panel halfway out would be lying about what is modal.
  </p>

  <h2>Handle follows edge</h2>
  <p>
    It is not cosmetic: a sash is pulled down from a bar across its roof, and a side panel is pulled sideways from a bar along its inside edge. A horizontal pill in a drawer announces the wrong gesture, and an affordance that lies is worse than having none.
  </p>

  <h2>On desktop it is not dragged</h2>
  <p>
    The handle is a <strong>tactile</strong> affordance: a bar that is pulled with the thumb. in a pointer
    There is nothing to pull that a click away or an ESC will not do better, so above the breakpoint of the
    system (<code>52rem</code>, the same width where the sidebar becomes a drawer and the dialog becomes
    sheet) the handle is hidden and the enhancer does not wire the drag. CSS and JS make the same cut, with the
    same <code>matchMedia</code>, so there is never a handle dangling at a width where it does not drag.
  </p>

  <h2>Motion: arriving, leaving and releasing are three intentions</h2>
  <p>
    Each one with its tokens, never an averaged duration: entering is announced, leaving is already decided. With <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> the <strong>panel continues to arrive at</strong>, the trip falls, not the Vaul, and the fade of the backdrop is what says that the page was inert. The drag is not touched: it is direct manipulation, not motion that the system reproduces for you.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="/en/transparency"><code>prefers-reduced-transparency</code></a> does not remove the backdrop: it makes it opaque. The modality and direct response to drag remain.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Releasing</strong> is the third, and its own: a released panel is not coming out, it is ending the momentum that the hand already gave it. That is why it does not consume <code>enter</code> or <code>exit</code>, both are short because no one expects them, but rather the <code>release</code> intent, with a long duration on purpose: cutting it is what makes the panel stop suddenly under your finger instead of continuing on. The same attempt for both destinations: returning home and leaving completely are the same shot with a different ending. It is the only intent that under <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> he cannot let go of his ride, the panel is where his finger left it and he has to get somewhere, so he lets go of the ride and lands short.
  </p>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={import_} label="CSS" />

  <StylingHooks groups={[{ file: "patterns/vaul.css", probe: "sk-vaul" }]} />
</Base>

<style is:global>
  /* The demo's skin belongs to the consumer, as always: the pattern brings hooks and structure, not
     paint. Width is decided by the pattern's clamp; height comes from the content up to the sheet's
     ceiling.
     `is:global`: the authored HTML lives inside the srcdoc and doesn't carry Astro's scope attribute;
     the adapter copies this global rule to the frame, a scoped rule couldn't reach it. */
  .demo-vaul {
    --sk-vaul-radius: var(--radius-surface) var(--radius-surface) 0 0;
    /* claim a real slice of the screen rather than hugging three rows at the bottom edge */
    min-block-size: min(64dvh, 32rem);

    /* The sheet's side inset, in ONE single place: the header, the link row, the list rows, and the
       actions all have to land on the same vertical line, and they're four separate rules.
       A hair above `md` and not `lg`: in a narrow sheet the jump to `lg` reads as lost width, while
       these 4px only pull the text off the edge. */
    --demo-vaul-inset: calc(var(--space-inset-md) + var(--space-inset-xs));
  }

  /* The sheet IS the column: header, link row, list, and actions are its direct children. An
     inner wrapper would only exist to redeclare the same flex and to add a padding the rows
     already carry. */
  .demo-vaul[open] {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }

  /* A sheet is held with one hand: every row of padding is a row of content that didn't fit.
     That's why the inset is short and not `xl`, and why the bands are separated with a line
     instead of a large `gap` — a line costs 1px and says the same thing.
     Up top it's worth paying for: the handle is a 12px bar with no air of its own, and with the
     header stuck to it the title looked like it was hanging off the edge instead of starting the sheet. */
  .demo-vaul__header {
    display: flex;
    gap: var(--space-inline-md);
    align-items: flex-start;
    justify-content: space-between;
    padding: var(--space-inset-sm) var(--demo-vaul-inset);
  }

  .demo-vaul__heading {
    display: grid;
    gap: 2px;
    min-inline-size: 0;
  }

  .demo-vaul__title {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--font-size-heading-sm);
    line-height: var(--font-line-height-heading);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-heading);
  }

  .demo-vaul__meta {
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    font-size: var(--font-size-caption);
  }

  .demo-vaul__link {
    flex: none;
    padding: var(--space-inset-sm) var(--demo-vaul-inset);
    border-block: 1px solid var(--color-border-subtle);
  }

  /* The list takes the remaining height and scrolls on its own: header, link, and actions stay
     put while the finger scrolls through the people.
     The side inset is NOT set here: the rows already carry their own, so the sheet's is passed to
     them through its hook and they keep running edge to edge. Padding on the list would pull them
     inward and leave the divider short. */
  .demo-vaul__people {
    --sk-list-row-padding-x: var(--demo-vaul-inset);

    flex: 1;
    min-block-size: 0;
    overflow-y: auto;
    overscroll-behavior: contain;
  }

  .demo-vaul__actions {
    display: flex;
    gap: var(--space-inline-sm);
    justify-content: flex-end;
    padding: var(--space-inset-sm) var(--demo-vaul-inset);
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--color-border-subtle);
  }
</style>
